# 5. Step 4 — Implement three deliberate exploration scales

The experience must have clear navigation modes rather than one continuous camera range with no change in information density.

## Mode A: Regional South Pole

Purpose: understand the polar region and compare candidates.

- Show the south-polar terrain and its geographic context.
- Show candidate markers with limited labels.
- Show a clear selected-site indicator.
- Keep the camera stable and the terrain recognizable.
- Allow the user to select a candidate directly.
- Show the verified terrain-data coverage boundary when relevant.
- Do not crowd the region with all scientific vectors, rings, and labels.

## Mode B: Candidate Overview

Purpose: inspect one candidate and its surroundings.

- Center the camera on the selected site.
- Keep enough terrain visible to understand the local crater, ridge, or regional context.
- Reduce unrelated candidate markers and labels.
- Display a compact site information panel.
- Make the selected coordinates accessible.
- Provide a clear transition into Local Inspection.
- Keep camera movement centered on the selected site unless the user deliberately pans.

## Mode C: Local Inspection

Purpose: inspect supported local terrain and illumination.

- Use an oblique view by default.
- Allow the user to orbit around the selected location.
- Preserve stable orientation and predictable controls.
- Show local terrain at the best available supported resolution.
- Keep only the overlays needed for local inspection.
- Offer a way to toggle terrain, illumination, and scientific vectors independently.
- Display terrain source, coverage, and resolution when available.
- Clearly label geometry-only views when terrain data does not support a detailed inspection.

The interface must visibly indicate the current mode.

Provide a reliable way to return to Candidate Overview or Regional South Pole without losing the selected site or UTC epoch.

Do not use abrupt camera jumps between modes. Use bounded, interruptible transitions.

