# 6. Step 5 — Fix deep zoom and camera reliability

Investigate the current camera-control implementation before changing it.

Do not simply increase `maxDistance`, decrease `minDistance`, or remove all zoom limits.

## Zoom requirements

- Support progressive zoom from global context to the deepest supported local scale.
- Calculate reasonable limits from scene scale, camera field of view, target scale, terrain coverage, and available geometry.
- Keep the camera's near and far clipping planes appropriate for the current viewing range.
- Avoid clipping terrain at close range.
- Avoid z-fighting and depth precision problems.
- Avoid camera penetration into the Moon or terrain.
- Preserve useful depth and perspective.
- Keep zoom speed consistent across scale changes.
- Prevent the camera from jumping when a new target is selected.
- Allow zoom toward the pointer when reliably supported, or toward the selected-site target.
- Make it possible to zoom back out from every valid state.

## Important distinction

Camera zoom is not terrain detail.

When a user zooms closer than the source data can resolve:
- Do not invent small craters or elevation variation.
- Do not exaggerate normal maps to imply measured relief.
- Do not imply centimeter- or meter-scale accuracy unless the dataset supports it.
- Communicate the limitation in the site inspector or terrain metadata panel.
- Consider limiting the deepest useful inspection scale based on verified data resolution.

## Orbit requirements

- Orbit remains functional at the deepest supported zoom.
- Maintain a stable camera target.
- Support polar orientations without singularities.
- Avoid camera flips and sudden changes in the up vector.
- Keep pointer gestures responsive.
- Separate orbit, pan, zoom, and click-to-select interactions.
- Handle pointer cancellation and lost pointer capture.
- Prevent accidental site selection at the end of a drag.
- Restore interaction after a fullscreen change, resize, or interrupted gesture.
- Ensure site selection does not trigger a competing camera animation.

If the camera target is updated, preserve a coherent camera position and orientation.

Use the existing camera-control library's documented capabilities rather than creating a second competing control system.

