# 9. Step 8 — Performance and lifecycle hardening

Measure actual bottlenecks before optimizing.

Investigate:
- Multiple active animation loops.
- Unnecessary React rerenders during camera motion.
- Excessive device pixel ratio.
- Expensive shadows and post-processing.
- Excessive draw calls and triangle counts.
- Repeated geometry/material/texture creation.
- Expensive work triggered on every pointer event.
- Recomputing ephemerides when the inputs have not changed.
- Heavy asset decoding or upload on the main thread.
- Unreleased WebGL resources.
- Duplicate event listeners.
- Observer or control instances surviving scene teardown.
- Excessive memory use after repeated fullscreen transitions.

Implement evidence-supported improvements.

Requirements:
- One active render loop per scene.
- Reuse resources where practical.
- Keep high-frequency camera updates outside React state when appropriate.
- Update UI diagnostics at a controlled cadence.
- Avoid regenerating terrain or textures during ordinary camera movement.
- Dispose resources and listeners when their owning scene is destroyed.
- Correctly resize renderer, camera aspect ratio, and controls.
- Pause or reduce nonessential rendering when the tab is hidden where appropriate.
- Resume correctly when the tab becomes visible.
- Provide graceful loading and failure states.
- Avoid quality changes that visibly oscillate or cause constant terrain popping.

Fullscreen must not cause the scene to mount twice or create another render loop.

If adaptive rendering quality is implemented, document its thresholds and ensure it never changes the scientific geometry or displayed measurements.

