# 12. Step 11 — Performance acceptance criteria

Establish a baseline before implementation and compare after implementation on the same machine, browser, viewport, and scene.

Measure:
- Median frame time.
- 95th-percentile frame time.
- Long frames during camera movement.
- Draw calls and triangles.
- Texture and geometry resource counts where available.
- Time to usable scene.
- Interaction latency where measurable.
- Performance before and after repeated fullscreen transitions.

Use approximately 16.7 ms per frame as the reference budget for 60 FPS, not as a universal hardware guarantee.

Do not report only average FPS.

Separate:
- Startup and asset-loading stalls.
- GPU rendering bottlenecks.
- Main-thread computation.
- Camera-control/input latency.
- Progressive memory or resource degradation.

Acceptance:
- No reproducible control freeze at the supported local zoom.
- No progressive slowdown after repeated interactions caused by leaked resources or duplicate loops.
- No broken camera after fullscreen or resize.
- No visual degradation caused by an arbitrary quality reduction without documented justification.
- Any remaining performance limitations are reproducible and explicitly documented.

Do not fabricate measurements when profiling APIs or browser access are unavailable.

