# 13. Step 12 — Scientific acceptance criteria

The 3D scene must clearly distinguish visual representation from validated science.

Verify:
- The selected site maps to the correct geographic coordinates.
- The site marker is positioned using the documented coordinate transformation.
- The selected site and UTC epoch are shared across the scene and linked views.
- Sun direction is calculated in the correct reference frame.
- Local solar elevation and azimuth use the appropriate local frame.
- Terrain coverage and resolution are based on actual asset metadata.
- Local terrain inspection does not imply greater accuracy than the source data supports.
- Earth geometric line of sight is not labeled as RF connectivity.
- Terrain obstruction is reported only when a valid terrain-aware calculation exists.
- Unknown inputs do not silently become valid-looking scientific outputs.

Where possible, compare scientific calculations against an independent reference implementation at several epochs and locations.

Report the reference method, coordinate frame, time convention, test inputs, residuals, tolerances, and limitations.

Do not treat visual alignment as independent scientific validation.

